    "created_at": "2017-03-03T16:22:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-03-03T16:22:27Z",
    "body": "Sure, but then what? Say library 1 adds its stuff first. Then library 2 loads, sees library 1 is already there, and add its stuff to library 1's `<menu>`. Now library 1 is toggled off and it removes its context menu, as well as library 2's. So now the library maker has to go one step further and assume that any other library could potentially add elements to their `<menu>` item. Any subsequent libraries may also want to add a `<hr>` between their menu items and previously defined menu items. So now library 1 has to keep track of which elements in its `<menu>` it actually added, and to be nice they should also check for a `<hr>` after their elements and remove that too. This assumes that any time a library adds elements to the `<menu>` they are appending them.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
